Every try bounded, several servers, exit codes that name the failure, a
polite resend, and SNTP without floating point.
- rdate.c: each try runs under an alarm of -t seconds (default 15), name
  lookup included, so a dead name server or a silent network cannot hang
  it; -r retries (default 1); every host given is tried in order.  Exit
  codes in rdate.h; one stderr line per failed host.  Upstream's -t
  (maximum round trip, msec) is -d.  -a steps a difference over -b
  seconds (default 60).  The "adjust local clock by" line names the
  address and is printed with -v and -p too.  -c (leap seconds from
  zoneinfo/right/UTC, which Amix lacks) is gone.  A refused reply is not
  retried.
- ntp.c: upstream sends a new query every 0.6 s, up to 25 a try, each with
  a new cookie, so a reply slower than 0.6 s is rejected and never counts.
  Here a silent server is asked again after 2 s, then 4 s, and not once
  under 2 s of the try are left; a reply to any datagram of the try counts,
  and the first good one ends it.  NTP timestamps stay 64-bit fixed point
  (32.32 since 1900); offsets are signed differences, right within 68
  years and across 2036.  Stratum 0 (kiss-o'-death) and leap indicator 3
  are refused (code 5).  The cookie comes from /dev/urandom (no arc4random
  here).
- rfc868time.c: returns a code instead of exiting.

